  "query_preview": "..\nTonight through Friday night: A closed upper level low pressure \ncirculation center over northern California today is progged to \neject northeast into eastern Oregon tomorrow. Above average \ntemperatures with increasing moisture will result in an increasingly \nunstable atmosphere for the afternoon and evening hours across \nsoutheast Washington into the southern to central Idaho Panhandle. \nIn addition, a dynamic shortwave disturbance will round the upper \nlevel low to provide a kicker to get convection going. This \nconvection will initiate south of the region across eastern Oregon \nand the southern Idaho Panhandle by late morning. Sunny skies out \nahead of the advancing disturbance will destabilize lower levels of \nthe atmosphere with surface based CAPE values in the range of 500-\n1000 J/kg across the Blue Mountains of northeast Oregon into \nthe Clearwaters by the early afternoon. This will be the focus \nof stronger surface based convection, and the Northeast Blue \nMountains of Washington and the Camas Prairie will be on the \nnorthern fringe of the northern extent of this potential. Mid \nlevel lapse rates will be fairly steep though and elevated \nconvection with that will also lead to the potential for \nthunderstorms, but less likely to become strong. The northern \nextent for thunderstorm activity will be across the Palouse to \nthe Silver Valley. Downdraft CAPE of 800-1000 J/kg will mean \nthat outflow winds will be the primary concern with convection \ntomorrow. The HRRR model indicates outflow winds of up to 35-40 \nmph with convection, and these gusts may extend far out away \nfrom the parent thunderstorm(s). This may result in blow dust \nwith rapid changes to visibility across southeast Washington. \nSecondary hazards will include brief downpours, small hail, and \noccasional cloud to ground lightning strikes.\n\nConvective potential will decrease into the late evening and \novernight hours, but at least a 30-40% chance for nocturnal showers \nwill continue through the night into early Friday. The upper level \nlow will migrate into southwest Idaho by Friday afternoon. \nP-wats over the southeast portion of the forecast area will \ndecrease a bit from around 0.9-1.0 inches on Thursday to around\n0.8 inches on Friday. Best dynamics will also shift away from \nthe region across eastern Idaho into southwest Montana. \nPotential for convection on Friday will decrease as a result and\nmore likely to be on the southeast periphery of the forecast \narea by Friday afternoon.\n\nSaturday through Wednesday: The remainder of the extended portion of \nthe forecast looks drier. The Inland Northwest will be under a flat \nridge to drier westerly flow. Temperatures will be seasonably warm \nwith highs in the upper 70s to around 80 for most valley locations \nthrough Monday. There is high confidence for these warm temperatures \nwith sunny to mostly sunny skies through the weekend. A trough of \nlower pressure looks to dig across the eastern Pacific the middle of \nnext week, but there is uncertainty with how close in proximity it \nwill be to the region. Temperatures look to cool 4-6 degrees from \nMonday to Tuesday and Wednesday. This will be down to near seasonal \naverages. /SVH",
